 13 the lead flipped sides almost every move, with Opus 5.5 making 11 blunders to the 12k's 9. The last swing went to White: at turn 33 Opus 5.5 turned a +19.1 lead into -11.8, and the endgame never moved it back.", "moments": [{"turn": 13, "text": "Opus 5.5 played E3 with a +3.6 lead and dropped to -3.0, a loss of 6.6 points. The engine preferred C2. It was the first big loss in a game that had been quiet and close, and it began the seesaw."}, {"turn": 18, "text": "The 12k's C5 lost 13.0 points, taking the lead from -8.1 to +4.9 in Black's favour. The engine wanted H5. White's blunders were as large as Black's in this stretch, and each one handed the lead straight back."}, {"turn": 28, "text": "The 12k played E7, which cost 30.9 points. Black's lead went from -14.5 to +16.4, the biggest swing of the game. The engine's choice was F7. White had been well ahead, and this move gave it all away."}, {"turn": 33, "text": "Opus 5.5 answered with its own E7, also a 30.9-point loss. The lead went from +19.1 to -11.8, and the engine's choice was H6. Black had been winning by a wide margin and never got back above -7.7 afterwards. This is the move that decided the game."}, {"turn": 45, "text": "The endgame was tidy. Opus 5.5 played J3, the engine's own choice, and the lead moved by only 0.1. The last twelve or so moves each lost 3.0 points or less, so the -8.0 deficit was set earlier."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "libs.py only parses a pasted board and prints each group's stones and liberty count with a DANGER flag, and count.py only counts stones and enclosed empty regions to give a territory score (optionally treating named stones as dead). Both work on the current position, neither proposes, ranks or looks ahead at moves, so no move search occurred.", "stop": false}}